 (lan-b) end-to-end\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"curl from node3 to http://10.10.3.4:8080/ succeeds and returns content both before and after all node restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c08a41dddfb9432b\",\"cmd-850f06304e184fe8\",\"cmd-8625cf01bd944ad7\",\"cmd-692f0132784b43da\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"First SNAT hop occurs on node1 (VyOS) as traffic enters the transit network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"nftables masquerade rule on eth1 for source 10.10.1.0/24 is active and conntrack directly shows node3's 10.10.1.3 translated to node1's transit address 10.10.2.1 for the live connection to node4:8080; rule and translation persist after node1 restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1288b52e18f14cf8\",\"cmd-a12ac1b9c45e4db2\",\"cmd-1eaf226c9ace4d62\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Second SNAT hop occurs on node2 (OPNsense) as traffic enters lan-b\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"pf outbound NAT on vtnet2 (lan-b) is active and pf state directly shows the transit-translated source 10.10.2.1:44480 further translated to node2's lan-b address 10.10.3.254:21839 on the way to node4:8080; rule and NAT persist after node2 restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c62d82360ff4477b\",\"cmd-b6a1f013759c4e53\",\"cmd-17ee1d7c577348cc\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"node4 records the connection as arriving from node2 (10.10.3.254), not from node3 or node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node4's HTTP service access log records source 10.10.3.254 (node2's lan-b address) for requests originating from node3, confirmed both before restarts and repeatedly after all four node restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-775bc803c0654001\",\"cmd-0139275a6c2d41ae\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart protocol: healthy baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state for node1, node2, node3, node4\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each node shows a pre-restart baseline (interfaces/rules/service state plus boot id), an explicit reboot invocation, a directly observed change in boot identity (node1 01e92881->19f34b03; node2 kern.boottime 06:36:44->06:44:18; node3 cfdbaef8->d186af40; node4 8d1d94e2->2e40da46), and post-restart confirmation of restored addressing/NAT/service and successful end-to-end curl. node4 required a corrective fix (moved served content from tmpfs-backed /tmp/www to persistent /srv/www) after discovering data loss, which was applied and re-verified.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d28f82a6b47948c5\",\"cmd-2379e87201e14d0b\",\"cmd-a19a61950fd74ac1\",\"cmd-1eaf226c9ace4d62\",\"cmd-c4e16ff6f0974d90\",\"cmd-ed38424aad36454e\",\"cmd-5657b470e1ae432f\",\"cmd-17ee1d7c577348cc\",\"cmd-5b68529a042341c8\",\"cmd-cfe7912b7b2c4ea2\",\"cmd-2122e5df1a1e4bd7\",\"cmd-463252e52f4e4742\",\"cmd-9008d55c27764466\",\"cmd-ac327fc7445a4d80\",\"cmd-cc6b1a44e9264898\",\"cmd-004b7deced7144aa\",\"cmd-e59fa6ef6d9e45a7\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.88,\"summary\":\"The executor performed a bounded, reversible topology-discovery phase using temporary test addresses (192.0.2.x) on node1/node2/node3/node4 to determine real NIC pairing; these were all explicitly removed/replaced once the real addressing was applied (cmd-b1ac3b5f48bb4f82, cmd-cecc48cf244247af, cmd-a980c5e89bc34966, and node1's 'delete interfaces ethernet ...' inside cmd-92a93e7174824adf), leaving no residue. All other state-changing commands (VyOS interface/route/NAT config, OPNsense config.xml interface/filter/NAT additions, systemd-networkd drop-ins on node3/node4, node4 HTTP service unit) map directly to satisfying the task's addressing, NAT, or service-persistence requirements. Minor residue: OPNsense config.xml backup files (.bak1/.bak2) and Python helper scripts (/tmp/addifs.py, /tmp/addrules.py) were left on node2, and node4's now-abandoned /tmp/www content was superseded by /srv/www without deletion; these are low-risk, non-destructive, and did not weaken any security control. No failed services, broken package state, or unsafe exposure were observed after the executor finished.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b1ac3b5f48bb4f82\",\"cmd-cecc48cf244247af\",\"cmd-a980c5e89bc34966\",\"cmd-92a93e7174824adf\",\"cmd-9922ba65c0d24351\",\"cmd-3af3ad9514f34620\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node4:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built and directly verified a working double-NAT path: node3 -> node1 (SNAT to transit address 10.10.2.1) -> node2 (SNAT to lan-b address 10.10.3.254) -> node4:8080, with node4's access log, node1 conntrack, and node2 pf state all corroborating the two translation hops and the final observed source. All four managed nodes were restarted one at a time with directly observed boot-identity changes and confirmed post-restart recovery of addressing, NAT rules, service state, and end-to-end connectivity, including a transparent corrective fix for node4's tmpfs-backed web content. Topology-discovery mutations were bounded and fully reverted.\",\"limitations\":[\"Node2's outbound NAT rule addition and firewall reload commands returned no direct textual confirmation of rule installation at the time (cmd-3463ec7297de400c) — final correctness was instead confirmed indirectly via the subsequent pfctl state/rule dump and successful traffic, which is sufficient but slightly less direct.\",\"Backup files (config.xml.bak1/.bak2) and helper scripts left on node2, and superseded /tmp/www content on node4, were not explicitly confirmed as cleaned up in the trace; these are low-risk but represent minor residue.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node3 (lan-a) can open connections to node4:8080 (lan-b) end-to-end","status":"satisfied","summary":"curl from node3 to http://10.10.3.4:8080/ succeeds and returns content both before and a